/target

# `mdbook build` output. The site is built by CI from docs/ on every push to
# main and served from GitHub Pages; nothing rendered is ever committed.
/book

# Editor/tool configs
.idea/
.vscode/
.zed/
.claude/

# testenv/ holds the config for the throwaway experience the ops subcommands
# are developed against; prodread/ reads the live one. Anything holding a real
# key is not committed.
testenv/.secrets/
testenv/*.lock.toml
prodread/.secrets/
prodread/*.lock.toml

# The configs themselves, because to be useful they have to hold real values: a
# real public IP in the key allowlists, real universe and place ids. Scrubbing
# them and committing placeholders worked, but only by discipline: the moment
# somebody pastes their own IP back in to actually run something, a tracked
# file holds personal data and is one `git commit -a` from being published.
#
# What is committed instead is the `.example` next to each one, which keeps
# every comment (the scope reasoning, and the rule that nothing in prodread/
# may ever hold a write scope). Copy it, fill it from .local/real-ids.toml, and
# git stops being able to see the result.
#
# Listed as a pattern rather than file by file: `rbx meta init`, `rbx shop
# init` and `rbx config pull` each write another `rbx*.toml` here the moment
# somebody exercises them against the test universe, and a rule that has to be
# extended for every new command is a rule that will be one command behind. The
# `.example` files are the exception, since they are the committed half.
testenv/rbx*.toml
!testenv/*.example.toml
prodread/rbx*.toml
!prodread/*.example.toml

# Python bytecode from scripts/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch sheet with real universe ids and personal notes. Not for the repo.
TRY-IT.md

# The real identifiers the committed configs carry placeholders for: public IP
# for the key allowlists, and the live universe/place ids. They are personal
# and operational data, so they live here and are copied into testenv/ and
# prodread/ by hand when running against real Open Cloud. Never tracked.
.local/

# Local copies written before an overwrite, and profile templates used for
# ad-hoc testing. Neither belongs to the repository.
*.backup.json
testenv/playerdata.template.json

# Place files used to exercise `rbx place` against the throwaway universe. A
# .rbxl is an opaque binary that can carry anything that was in the place when
# it was saved, so it is ignored by extension rather than by name: the point is
# that no future one gets committed either.
*.rbxl
*.rbxlx

# staging area for the extracted crate, never committed here
extracted/
